"Links category values across two positions to reveal changes in levels and relative order.\n\nUse when: A modest number of entities are compared at two common occasions. Rank reversals are important alongside numeric changes.\n\nAvoid when: Endpoint labels or many crossings make individual trajectories unreadable.\n\nVisual grammar: Vertical position encodes the same quantitative scale on both sides. Line direction encodes change; crossings encode order reversals.\n\nBuild: Place the two occasions on separate vertical rails with identical scales. Draw one segment per matched entity using its measured endpoints. Place short labels beyond both rails and separate labels from line endpoints.\n\nCheck: Check both rails share limits, units, and tick spacing. Verify every segment matches the same entity across occasions. Disclose missing entities and do not silently drop difficult labels.\n\nPitfalls: Crossings are intrinsic evidence of rank reversals; never move values to remove them. Prefer dumbbell rows by default when tracking individual changes matters more than rank.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic district participation in two years; label endpoints and highlight only three focal districts.\n\nAlternatives: Dumbbell chart, Bump chart, Indexed line chart."
